  <Document>
    <name>🌙 LIVE ATMOSPHERIC VISIBILITY</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 7, 2026 10:00AM EDT</p><h2>LIVE ATMOSPHERIC VISIBILITY</h2><p><b>Gradient key</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/visibility/legend.png" width="600" alt="gradient key"><br>Meteorological visibility in statute miles (HRRR surface analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>LOWEST 0 Obscured</b> maroon &rarr; very poor &rarr; poor &rarr; moderate yellow &rarr; good green &rarr; very good cyan &rarr; blue &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 30 Crystal clear</b> deep blue. The key steps evenly (0/5/10/15/20/25/30) with observing quality written under each value. Same visibility always shows the same color; above 30 mi clamps into deep blue. Human-observer/mariner/pilot visibility through haze, mist, precipitation, and smoke — not astronomical seeing.</p><p>This entry auto-refreshes from the live overlay (source: NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km surface visibility analysis, hourly cycles).</p><p>Turn on/off independently of all other layers. Add this file once; new source cycles appear automatically.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <NetworkLink>
      <name>🌙 LIVE ATMOSPHERIC VISIBILITY — auto-refresh</name>
